## Enable OpenSSH with key-only logins

```nix
{
  services.openssh = {
    enable = true;
    settings = {
      PasswordAuthentication = false;
      KbdInteractiveAuthentication = false;
      # PubkeyAuthentication = true;  # already the default, shown for clarity
    };
  };
}
```

- `services.openssh.enable = true` starts `sshd` (and opens the port in the firewall).
- `settings` is passed straight into `sshd_config`, so you use the standard OpenSSH option names.
- **Both** `PasswordAuthentication = false` and `KbdInteractiveAuthentication = false` matter: with PAM enabled, keyboard-interactive can fall back to password auth, so disabling only the first one can leave a password path open. (`ChallengeResponseAuthentication` is the old name for the second.)

**Before you apply:** make sure your key is in `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` on the machine (and that you have a console/other access path), so you don't lock yourself out. If you want to be stricter, you can also add `AllowUsers`/`AllowGroups` in `settings`.

How I know: this is the standard NixOS `services.openssh` module interface — I could not verify the option names against the nixpkgs source in this session (see gaps).

