Here's the standard NixOS way, in your configuration (e.g. `configuration.nix`):

```nix
{
  pkgs,
  ...
}:
{
  users.users.developer = {
    isNormalUser = true;
    description = "Developer";
    home = "/home/developer";
    shell = pkgs.zsh;
    groups = [ "users" "wheel" ];
  };
}
```

Then `nixos-rebuild switch` (or `switch --flake .#host`).

What each part does:

- `isNormalUser = true` — marks it a regular (non-system) user: creates `/home/developer`, sets a default shell, and adds the user to the `users` group.
- `shell = pkgs.zsh` — overrides the default `bash` with zsh.
- `groups = [ "users" "wheel" ]` — `wheel` is the sudo group on NixOS: the default sudoers file grants `%wheel` passwordless sudo, so membership is what gives sudo privileges. Note that `groups` *replaces* the default list, so keep `"users"` in it if you want both.

Optional extras:

- `openssh.enable = true` on the user if you want SSH login.
- `password` (hashed) if you want a password set at creation.

How I know: this is the documented NixOS users module behaviour (`users.users.<name>.isNormalUser`, `shell`, `groups`) and the default sudoers rule for `wheel`. I could not verify it against source here — see gaps.

