The mechanism is the `follows` keyword on a flake input. In your `flake.nix`:

```nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-24.05";

    mydep = {
      url = "github:owner/mydep";
      inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";   # mydep's nixpkgs now follows yours
    };
  };
}
```

`inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs"` tells Nix that `mydep`'s `nixpkgs` input is not its own — it follows your top-level `nixpkgs` input, so both resolve to the same flake. Notes:

- `follows` can point at any input path, including nested ones: `inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "otherdep.nixpkgs"`.
- It only redirects inputs the dependency actually declares. If `mydep` has no `nixpkgs` input, the `follows` has nothing to attach to.
- If you just need to *use* your nixpkgs when calling into a dependency (rather than pinning its input), you can pass it explicitly in the flake body, e.g. `mydep.lib.foo (inputs.nixpkgs.legacyPackages.x86_64-linux)`.

How I know: this is the standard flake `follows` feature from the Nix manual's flake inputs section. I could not verify it against the manual or source in this session — `recall`, `web_fetch` and `clone` were all unavailable — so treat the exact syntax as from memory.

