Here's the standard NixOS way to do it:

```nix
{
  pkgs, ... }:

{
  # The user
  users.users.developer = {
    isNormalUser = true;
    description = "Developer";
    home = "/home/developer";
    shell = pkgs.zsh;
    extraGroups = [ "wheel" "networkmanager" ];
  };

  # Make zsh available system-wide (also gives you /etc/zshrc, completions, etc.)
  programs.zsh.enable = true;
}
```

Key points:

- **`isNormalUser = true`** — creates the user with a home directory and a sane default shell; NixOS manages the account.
- **`shell = pkgs.zsh`** — sets zsh as the login shell. Enabling `programs.zsh` is the recommended companion so zsh is properly installed and configured.
- **`extraGroups = [ "wheel" ]`** — this is what gives sudo privileges on NixOS: the default sudoers file grants `wheel` members full sudo. `networkmanager` is a common extra for network management.
- **`description`** — a human-readable name shown in greeters and `getent passwd`.

After `nixos-rebuild switch`, the user exists and can run `sudo` immediately. If you want a password, set `users.users.developer.initialPassword = "..."` (it's set once, on first creation) or use `passwd developer` after the rebuild.

One caveat: I couldn't verify this against the nixpkgs source in this session (web fetch and clone were unavailable), so the details above are from general NixOS knowledge — the `wheel`-group sudoers line in particular is the one thing worth double-checking against your `nixos/modules/config/sudo.nix` if you want to be certain.

