Here's the standard NixOS way — add this to your configuration (e.g. `configuration.nix` or a module in your flake):

```nix
{
  pkgs,
  ...
}:
{
  users.users.developer = {
    isNormalUser = true;
    description = "Developer";
    home = "/home/developer";
    shell = pkgs.zsh;
    extraGroups = [ "wheel" ];
  };
}
```

Then apply it with `nixos-rebuild switch` (or `nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#host`).

What each part does:

- `isNormalUser = true` — marks it as a regular (non-system) user: creates the user in the `users` group, sets up `/home/developer` with a home profile, and defaults the shell to bash (which we override below).
- `shell = pkgs.zsh` — sets the login shell to zsh.
- `extraGroups = [ "wheel" ]` — this is what grants sudo. NixOS's default sudoers configuration allows members of the `wheel` group to run sudo (with a password, per the default `security.sudo.wheelNeedsPassword = true`).
- `description` and `home` are optional — `home` defaults to `/home/<username>` and `description` defaults to the username.

If you also want an SSH key for the user, add `openssh.enableEd25519KeyGeneration = true;` to the same block.

